WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:07.040
Would you please be so kind to introduce yourself to the audience of the Nexus Institute?

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.390
Where do you come from?

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:14.160
What is your background?

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.990
Thanks so much Rob, it is a pleasure to be
here with you.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.460
I am an American philosopher.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:26.960
My degrees are from Stanford and from Harvard
where I worked with people like John Rawls,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.120
Robert Nozick and Tim Scanlon.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.500
The great days of Harvard philosophy.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:38.460
My career has been in Britain and I am now
the Chair of Philosophy and Law at King's

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:39.820
College London School of Law.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:45.860
Being here at the heart of Silicon Valley...
will Silicon Valley be a part of the problem

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:51.240
or a part of the solution when it comes down
to the question 'What will save the world?'?

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.679
Humanity is moving from its old problems to
its new.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.770
The old problems were crises of ignorance.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.800
We did not understand the world well enough
to manipulate it and to solve our problems.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:11.060
300 years ago, we did not understand the plague. It wiped out a third, half of cities all over the world.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:17.980
Now we understand those kinds of problems. Ignorance no longer plagues us.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:24.800
Our new problems are not crises of ignorance. They are crises of our own invention.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:37.360
Our computers are so powerful, so present and so sapient we can hardly keep anything from them anymore.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:49.460
Our weapons are so mighty, so devastating, they threaten human life on the earth. Even climate change is a crisis of invention.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:02:01.200
All of these machines that we build, this
smoke coming off. All these people living longer, eating better, travelling more to see the world, to see each other.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:07.740
Is it not poignant that our inventions start
avalanching into this mortal danger

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:14.220
that might really be problems for our grandchildren. Those are the crises of invention.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:24.440
The hard things about the new crises are the
bads, so tightly wound up with the goods.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:34.460
How can we keep all the blessings of our technology
and our industry while not allowing them to threaten our very way of life?

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:43.780
How does that relate to the title of your
new book? I read it with great pleasure: 'Blood Oil'.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:55.000
There's a strange thing about our world: natural resources, which should be a blessing, are in so many places a curse.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:03.120
There is one statistic to sum it up. Think of all the progress the developing world
has made in the last generation

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:12.740
China, India, even Africa is doing so much better - except the countries that have oil.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:22.420
Countries that have oil are no richer, no
freer, no more peaceful than they were even in 1980.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:28.600
That is shocking. You could think of Iran, Iraq, Syria, Libya, Sudan...

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.489
Saudi Arabia.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:42.780
All of these countries are cursed by oil. And that is because of one very old, very
bad rule from the past which we have not reformed yet.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:55.700
The rule that basically says: for oil, might makes right. Whoever can control oil by force these days, our law says: we buy it from them.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:04:03.960
So, when Khadaffi took over Libia a long time ago, the world started to buying oil from him.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:13.080
And than years later, when rebel groups took
over the same wells from Khadaffi, it became legal for all of us to buy from the rebels.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:24.600
The rule says that whoever has the most guns will get a huge amount of money from the world and that money gives them absolute power.

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:29.080
Money comes in with no strings attached, it never has to be paid back.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:36.760
Of course the power is unavailable to the
people of the country who have to watch while their resources are sold off to the world.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:44.250
That money comes in and if it goes to rebels or to armed groups, they can use it to buy more weapons

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:52.000
If it goes to an authority, the authority
can use it to buy muscle and loyalty to stay in power.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:58.880
And if it is the Saudis who get the world's
money for oil, they use that money to

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:06.320
spread their extreme, intolerant version of
Islam around the world and that is the version of Islam that we now see.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:15.360
Mutated into jihadi violence, not only in
the Middle East and Asia but also in Europe and maybe even here in America.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:23.266
Does this also explain how it is possible
that on the one hand Saudi Arabia

00:05:23.266 --> 00:05:40.146
is a fertile ground for extremism spreading around the world in the shape of IS or other monsters and at the same time

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:50.560
- it is now April 2016 - president Obama is
paying his respects again to the king of Saudi Arabia

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:58.960
instead of standing up as he could stand up against Putin or Khadaffi.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.360
In the short term it always makes sense to do business as usual.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:10.720
In the short term it always makes sense to make an alliance with the authoritarian regime. How is that going?

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:18.320
How has that strategy worked in the past forty years? How did it go with the shah of Iran? How did it work with Khadaffi?

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:25.040
How did it work with Saddam? How did it work with Bashir in Sudan, Bashar [Assad] in Syria?

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:31.200
How is it working to try to keep the absolute power of oil in check by making alliances.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:37.750
You just told us that you have studied at
Harvard with the great professors there.

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:49.090
Obama also was a student at Harvard, Harvard Law School. Why is it that you understand something he apparently does not understand?

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:58.080
John Rawls said: 'A politician looks to the
next election. The statesman looks to the next generation.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:04.280
The philosopher looks to the permanent conditions of humanity and how they can be made better.'

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:11.120
Politicians always have looked to the short term. It is up to us as philosophers and as citizens

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:14.960
to look to the longer term and the world as a whole.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:19.680
Do we find a clue to what will save the world?

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:20.680
Yes.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:22.200
Tell us.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:29.480
The clue to saving the world is depth perception: seeing from both eyes at once.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:40.870
The world is a terrible place, compared to
what it should be: violence, extremism, climate change, resource management.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:50.480
But the world is a much better place than it was. We really are living better than we were even 60 years ago.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:59.260
The world is richer, human lives are longer, fewer women die in childbirth, literacy rates are

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:01.280
as high as they have ever been.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:10.660
More democracies in the world than ever, and, shocking as it seems, it is the most peaceful time in history.

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:14.680
Both of these things are true. The world is terrible compared to what it should be

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:17.200
but it is better than it was.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:23.100
Learning from the ways the world has gotten better can give us solutions for the problems to come

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:31.800
and get us to keep faith with our ancestors who struggled so hard to overcome their challenges

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.000
which were at least as critical as the ones that face us now

